>>
endobj
676 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (OAD BALANCING WITH OCTAVIA)
/E (OAD BALANCING WITH OCTAVIA)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
677 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Load balancing ensures that incoming network traffic is distributed evenly across multiple backend virtual machines \(VMs\). This avoids overloading any single instance and increases fault tolerance. In OpenStack, this functionality is provided by Octavia, a service dedicated to managing load balancers.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
678 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The process begins by creating a virtual load balancer that is assigned a floating IP address for external access. A listener is defined to handle incoming traffic on a specific port \(e.g., HTTP on port 80\). A pool of backend servers is then associated with the listener. Traffic is routed to these backend servers based on a load balancing algorithm, such as round robin. Health monitors are used to continuously check the status of each backend VM, ensuring that traffic is only sent to healthy instances.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
679 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (This setup enhances the reliability of web services by allowing the system to continue operating even if one or more instances fail.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
